  "answer": "Based on current web results retrieved in October 2026, water boils at the summit of Mount Everest at approximately 68 °C to 72 °C (154 °F to 162 °F), with 70 °C (158 °F) frequently cited as the standard representative boiling point [W1], [W2], [W3], [W4].\n\nThis significant decrease from the standard sea-level boiling point of 100 °C occurs because atmospheric pressure at the summit (altitude ~8,848–8,849 m) drops to approximately 337 hPa—roughly one-third (or about 34%) of sea-level pressure [W1], [W2], [W3], [W5]. Because boiling takes place when water's vapor pressure matches the ambient atmospheric pressure, water molecules at this high altitude require less thermal energy to escape into vapor [W1], [W3]. The exact boiling temperature at the summit can fluctuate slightly depending on local weather conditions and barometric changes [W1]."
